```apex
public with sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {

    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> failures = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        List<Contact> contacts = new List<Contact>();

        // Query the phone values for the supplied contacts.
        for (Contact c : [SELECT Id, Phone FROM Contact WHERE Id IN :contactIds]) {
            if (String.isNotBlank(c.Phone)) {
                // Remove every character that is not a digit or '+'.
                c.Phone = c.Phone.replaceAll('[^0-9+]', '');
                contacts.add(c);
            }
        }

        if (contacts.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        // Partial save so one bad record doesn't block the rest.
        Database.SaveResult[] results = Database.update(contacts, false);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            Database.SaveResult sr = results[i];
            if (!sr.isSuccess()) {
                failures.put(contacts[i].Id, sr.getErrors()[0].getMessage());
            }
        }

        return failures;
    }
}
```

Notes on where I adjusted your approach:

- **You said "loop over those contacts" but gave me a list of Ids.** An `Id` has no `Phone` field, so the method first queries the contacts (`SELECT Id, Phone ... WHERE Id IN :contactIds`) and loops over those records. One SOQL query for up to 200 Ids is bulk-safe.
- I used `String.isNotBlank(c.Phone)` rather than just `!= null` so contacts whose phone is an empty/whitespace string are also left out, matching the intent of "has a phone."
- The regex `[^0-9+]` strips everything that isn't a digit or `+`, which turns `+44 (20) 7946-0018` into `+442079460018` exactly as required. (Note it keeps any `+` in the middle too, e.g. `1+2` stays `1+2`; your spec only mentions a *leading* plus, but since you explicitly said "remove every character that is not a digit or `+`", I followed that literally rather than guessing at stricter rules.)
- Added an early return and an empty-list guard to avoid a needless DML call when there is nothing to update.
- Used `contacts[i]` positionally against `results[i]` because `Database.update(..., false)` returns results in the same order as the input list — that's how we map a failed result back to its contact Id.

